; A `.cbor` payload whose TARGET is a bare fixed value (`bytes .cbor 42`), in MEMBER / element
; position. ABORTS under the DEFAULT profile — a tracked-known gap, recorded here so a fix flips this
; row loudly rather than the class going unnoticed.
;
; The `Fixed` deserialize branch verifies a constant and evaluates to nothing, so it asserts its
; caller passed no before/after wrapper text (`generation/deserialize.rs`, the two `assert_eq!`s
; guarded by `if !cli.preserve_encodings`). A `.cbor` overload wraps its target's read in the
; payload's own staging expression, which IS before/after text — so the two meet and the assert
; fires. The asserts are the guard that caught this and are deliberately left in place: reaching
; them means a caller is asking a value-less branch to produce a value, which a silent widening
; would paper over.
;
; The class covers every fixed kind (`42`, `"s"`, `true`, `null`) and both the bare and the keyed
; element spellings — it keys on `Fixed` under a `.cbor` chain, not on the constant or the key.
; Two neighbours are deliberately NOT it:
;   - the rule-BODY spelling `x = bytes .cbor 42` is refused GRACEFULLY one seam earlier, by the
;     bare-fixed-value rule-body rejection, and never reaches the deserialize walk;
;   - `--preserve-encodings` generates it at exit 0, because the asserts are flag-guarded there and
;     the fixed branch takes its encoding-carrying path instead. That divergence is the sharpest
;     statement of the gap: the same spec is buildable under one profile and aborts under another.
;
; Surfaced by the recombination sweep (`outer=arr_single inner=cbor_payload filler=type2.value`)
; once the tagged-optional member kind reshuffled its seeded draws onto the composition; confirmed
; pre-existing by generating the minimal spelling at the parent commit.
x = [bytes .cbor 42]
